EXT. SALT FLAT - DAY

White to every horizon. A dead pickup sits hood-up, its shadow short. Heat shimmers.

MAREN LUND, 30s, sunburnt, wraps a rag around her wrist. DELPHINE ACHTERBERG, 40s, sits in the shade of the open tailgate, boots off, counting water bottles. She lines them up. Four.

OKAFOR, 50s, studies the engine, wrench in hand. He doesn't speak much.

Maren drops the rag. She walks to Delphine and she crouches. She nudges the bottles with a finger. She reaches for one and she is stopped by Delphine's hand.

DELPHINE
Four.

MAREN
Four's fine.

DELPHINE
Four is two days. Three if she stops complaining.

MAREN
Who?

DELPHINE
Pick one.

Okafor wipes his hands on his trousers. Closes the hood. It thuds.

OKAFOR
Fuel line's cracked.

MAREN
Fixable?

OKAFOR
With tape. Not with salt in it.

Maren stands. Delphine tilts her head. They share a look. Maren turns to the horizon. She shades her eyes. A glint far out, then nothing.

The radio inside the cab crackles. Okafor leans in through the window and keys it.

RANGER (V.O.)
(garbled)
Station Two-Four. Anyone on this channel.

OKAFOR
Two-Four, this is the Isuzu on the east track. We're stuck.

RANGER (V.O.)
Copy east track. Say position.

OKAFOR
Forty kilometres past the marker. White ground. No landmark.

RANGER (V.O.)
Copy. We'll send a truck at first light. Hold your position.

MAREN
First light's fourteen hours.

OKAFOR
Then we hold.

He releases the key. Static. Maren sits down in the sand beside the tailgate. Delphine hands her a bottle. Maren pushes it back.

MAREN
You first.

DELPHINE
I'm fine.

MAREN
You're not.

DELPHINE
She's not either.

Maren glances at Okafor. He's already looking at the sky. He pulls the tape from the glove box.

He kneels at the front wheel and begins to wind it around the line. Slow, careful. Neither of the women speaks. Wind shifts the salt in a long ribbon across the ground.
